course: dictionary
lesson: 12145
title: Comparatively
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Comparatively means when you compare one thing with another, or to some degree. It is a clue word. «The bus was comparatively empty.» Empty compared with what? Compared with a busy bus, which is full of people.
explain: The word does not give an exact amount. «The test was comparatively easy» means easy for this group of test takers, compared with other tests, and not that everyone found it simple. Read the sentence and ask yourself: compared with what?
explain: A good check is to add «compared with other things like it» after the word. If the sentence still makes sense, comparatively is the right word. «The new school is comparatively small» works, because it is small compared with other schools.
fact: Comparatively means when compared with something else, or to some degree.
fact: It often comes just before an adjective, such as «comparatively cheap» or «comparatively quiet».
fact: It tells the reader that a judgement depends on what you compare it with.
fact: «Comparatively» is an adverb, so it describes how something is, such as how cheap, quiet or small.
fact: Comparatively is used a lot in maths, science, history and geography to compare amounts, places and times.
exercise: «The shop was comparatively quiet this morning.» What does comparatively mean here? | The shop was closed | The shop was silent and empty forever | The shop was loud and busy | *Compared with other times, the shop was quiet
exercise: «Maths was comparatively easy for Priya, but spelling was hard.» What is Priya comparing? | The sun with the moon | *Maths with spelling | Today with yesterday | Her friends with her teacher
exercise: «The village is comparatively small.» Which word tells you the village is judged against something else? | is | *comparatively | village | small
test: «The hotel was comparatively cheap for a city hotel.» What does comparatively tell the reader? | *The price is judged against other city hotels | The hotel was very old | The hotel had no rooms | The hotel was free
test: Ben says, «Comparatively means exactly the same as completely.» Is Ben right? | *No, comparatively means compared with something else, not completely | Yes, the words are the same | Yes, both mean every single one | No, but both mean very tall
exam: In «Our garden is comparatively large», what must the reader ask themselves to understand the word? | How old is the garden? | *Large compared with what? | Who planted the garden? | Is the garden green?
